<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clara Schumann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Clara Wieck, Clara Josephine Wieck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clara Schumann, born Clara Wieck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, was a German pianist, composer and piano teacher whose 61-year concert career changed the piano recital by giving less room to purely virtuosic works. Trained as a child prodigy by her father Friedrich Wieck, she made her official debut at the Leipzig Gewandhaus in 1828 and was named Royal and Imperial Austrian Chamber Virtuoso in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1838. She married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> in 1840; they had eight children and encouraged the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a>, and she gave the public premieres of many works by both men. After Robert's death she toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for decades, often with Joseph Joachim, and from 1878 taught at Dr. Hoch's Konservatorium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clara-schumann.jpg" alt="Clara Schumann" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Hanfstaengl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Hanfstaengl_-_Clara_Schumann_(1857).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…emblem of Austria and its monarchy: Beethoven in a work celebrating the end of the Napoleonic Wars, Rossini, Paganini, Donizetti, <b>Clara Schumann</b>, Smetana and Bruckner, while Bartók used it in his symphonic poem Kossuth (1903) as the sign of the Austrian enemy of the Hungarian Revolution of 1848.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>49 in November 1868, and the lullaby was first performed in public on 22 December 1869 in Vienna, by the singer Luise Dustmann with <b>Clara Schumann</b> at the piano.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wiegenlied">Wiegenlied (Brahms's Lullaby)</a></li></ul></section>
